Your function doesn't stop because it lacks a base case. A base case is a condition that stops the recursion. Without it, the function will keep calling itself indefinitely, leading to a stack overflow.

Add a base case to stop the recursion when `n` is 0 or 1. For example:
```cpp
int fact(int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1) {
        return 1;
    }
    return n * fact(n - 1);
}
```

This way, when `n` reaches 0 or 1, the function will return 1, stopping the recursion.

Your code looks almost correct! The only issue is that you haven't handled the base case for the factorial function. The factorial of 0 is 1. Add this base case to your function.

Here's how you can fix it:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

// Return n! using recursion.
int fact(int n) {
    if (n == 0) {
        return 1;
    }
    return n * fact(n - 1);
}

int main() {
    std::cout << fact(5) << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

